response.value:
 with this, adjusting the IP to your node IP if needed:

```nginx
# Upstream Kubernetes ingress (HTTP on NodePort)
# Set these to your cluster's node IP and NodePort
map "" $k8s_node_ip    { default 192.168.1.3; }   # Node IP
map "" $k8s_node_port  { default 30333; }         # NodePort exposed by nginx-ingress
map "" $k8s_host       { default embeddings.192-168-1-3.nip.io; }  # Ingress host rule

server {
  listen 80;
  server_name embeddings-proxy.local;  # Change to the hostname you use for this proxy

  # Allow larger JSON payloads if needed (match ingress)
  client_max_body_size 20m;

  # Optional basic health check for the proxy itself
  location = /proxy-health {
    return 200 "ok";
    add_header Content-Type text/plain;
  }

  # Forward everything to the Kubernetes ingress
  location / {
    proxy_pass http://$k8s_node_ip:$k8s_node_port;

    # Preserve the Host header the K8s ingress uses for routing
    proxy_set_header Host $k8s_host;

    # Standard upstream headers
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

    # Keep-alive, buffering, and timeouts similar to your ingress annotations
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Connection "";
    proxy_buffering on;
    proxy_buffers 16 64k;
    proxy_buffer_size 64k;

    proxy_read_timeout 180s;
    proxy_send_timeout 180s;

    # Retry on transient upstream errors (mirrors ingress settings)
    proxy_next_upstream error timeout http_500 http_502 http_503 http_504 invalid_header;
    proxy_next_upstream_tries 2;
  }
}
```

Notes:
- The key line is `proxy_set_header Host $k8s_host;` — your K8s ingress rule matches on `embeddings.192-168-1-3.nip.io`, so we must send that Host header even if clients hit your outer proxy with a different hostname.
- If you want to parameterize the IP/port/host via env vars in your Docker container, you can map them from environment variables instead of the static map directive.

Example env-based substitution (optional):
- Replace the map lines with:
  - `set $k8s_node_ip $ENV_K8S_NODE_IP;`
  - `set $k8s_node_port $ENV_K8S_NODE_PORT;`
  - `set $k8s_host $ENV_K8S_INGRESS_HOST;`
- Then pass those env vars to the NGINX container.

## Quick verify

- Reload NGINX in your proxy container (choose one):
  - docker exec -it <nginx-container> nginx -s reload
  - docker compose exec nginx nginx -s reload
  - Or restart the proxy container

- Test through the proxy:
  - curl -s http://<your-proxy-host-or-ip>/health
  - You should see a 200 and the JSON body: {"status":"healthy", ...}

If you want me to apply this directly, share the current contents of `nginx.proxy.conf` or give me access to that workspace so I can patch it for you.

